The C project implements a chained hash trie ([38;5;10mchtrie[0m) — a trie data structure where children are stored in a hash table of linked lists. Here's what each function does:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mChtrie::new(n, m)[0m — Allocates a trie with at most [38;5;10mn[0m nodes and alphabet size [38;5;10mm[0m. Clamps both to ≥1, checks for overflow, computes hash table capacity as [38;5;10m(n-1) + (n-1)/3[0m, and initializes the index pool (root node 0 is pre-allocated, [38;5;10midxmax[0m starts at 1). Returns [38;5;10mNone[0m on overflow.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mChtrie::walk(from, sym, creat)[0m — Hashes [38;5;10mfrom * alphsz + sym[0m to find the bucket, then walks the chain looking for a matching edge. If [38;5;10mcreat != 0[0m and no match exists, allocates a new node index (from the recycled pool or incrementing [38;5;10midxmax[0m) and inserts a new edge at the head of the chain.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mChtrie::del(from, sym)[0m — Finds and removes the edge from the hash chain, recycling the freed node index back into the pool via [38;5;10mpush_back[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mChtrie::free()[0m — Clears all internal storage (Rust's [38;5;10mDrop[0m handles the actual deallocation).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mchtrie_walk[0m / [38;5;10mchtrie_del[0m — Free functions that delegate to the methods.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Key Rust adaptations:[0m[0m
